#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c981d0-879c-5395-961f-18d30c7ee1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491467} {:answer/id #uuid "30ccc38c-3c4b-51d7-a8be-015493817d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491550} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9407b6-9b59-518b-866e-5aec8e95de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{33}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491798} {:answer/id #uuid "cbceddc2-2b0f-51bf-9732-a4901abca037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491799} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c914c6-693e-5352-8b50-6792d0a0a2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c981d0-879c-5395-961f-18d30c7ee1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491467} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d0d86a-f951-5330-9b83-6ded6a5ca5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0a58b0a-c208-5232-9cb9-1df706de5400" :question/math-academy-id "q-34618" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(0)$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{2t}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{3t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,-2{e}^{2t}⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{0}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,-2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{9}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{3}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2870427b-9fcb-5fd7-b0fd-1a1a1b0dc4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491538} {:answer/id #uuid "353d2804-a43e-5df9-80ef-d3844280008d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491559} {:answer/id #uuid "7e064b2d-d342-5924-8c38-37c0f7e7f14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491676} {:answer/id #uuid "97265fa4-83a5-5552-82a3-d03e500d17ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491713} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f422f-ea71-5192-afa8-1cf02f8737c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a67f422f-ea71-5192-afa8-1cf02f8737c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c46275-3046-5183-a66a-80853ae0abe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6c5544d-5a76-5194-9e05-adccd06c8464" :question/math-academy-id "q-36223" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$,  given that the curve and its unit tangent $T(t)$ are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,1⟩,\\,T(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1060224c-d0b3-5776-a766-96b15404205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491485} {:answer/id #uuid "28349558-5db2-5c09-8b29-f853f31f0913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius is $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491537} {:answer/id #uuid "31280857-9970-5bf7-888c-fc39c357541b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(2,2)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491551} {:answer/id #uuid "5611b291-95d5-535d-999e-5155c671c2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-2,0)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491619} {:answer/id #uuid "910409ee-6695-5f67-a309-77a3fe4d5eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,2)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1060224c-d0b3-5776-a766-96b15404205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491485} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa7d73e-6b11-5043-84cc-72ac6b11b133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb82f4d9-80ff-57cb-8e48-0da61beda250" :question/math-academy-id "q-97585" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(2,2)$ where $t=2$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2},\\,\\frac{2-t}{2}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(2)=⟨2,2⟩,$ since the point $P(2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(2)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2-t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2-t}{2\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =⟨\\frac{2-2}{2\\sqrt{4(2)-{2}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=2$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2)+R(2)N(2) & =⟨2,2⟩+2⟨0,-1⟩=⟨2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff6569a-4cab-5e8f-8762-376041784c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} {:answer/id #uuid "35e1a2f8-2f8a-5d39-9327-ca2e3e8e0ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361077} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed54666-3b56-576a-9615-df8106a5c984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361398} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa0a898-43f7-561b-bd46-61b1497e96c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361406} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad7825-cc9e-5adb-aafe-2969fcf48b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff6569a-4cab-5e8f-8762-376041784c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90568e4-8415-56af-8bbd-a969ac099004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4602817c-a855-5ccd-8d1a-bca1a25a4651" :question/math-academy-id "q-97594" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8d1fdb-1e8d-5f02-9ea7-c3c298ae432f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361248} {:answer/id #uuid "af862e42-0cba-5667-86e1-bcba2137990c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361614} {:answer/id #uuid "c253a7fe-6f5f-512c-aff1-bf5aa8dd058e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361708} {:answer/id #uuid "ed37a445-de09-58c0-b587-33b13984c10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9e95f8-2048-5fb9-b5b3-a7a136bdb4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a53a3d-9f1a-5b06-bcde-4575632a0617" :question/math-academy-id "q-98665" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
